# The ledger of `containers`: what Go 1.27 (`container/list`, `container/ring`),
# Rust 1.100 (`BTreeMap`, `BTreeSet`, `VecDeque`, `LinkedList` and their
# cursors and entries) and Python 3.15 (`collections.deque`, `graphlib`,
# `functools.lru_cache`) offer for the structures the language's list and map
# do not make, one block per capability, and what Heroes answers to each. The
# census of Odin's `core:container`, Zig's std, Java's `java.util` and .NET's
# `System.Collections` that chose the package (the author, 2026-11-17) is named
# in the notes where it decided something.
#
# What other ledgers hold is not repeated: Go's `container/heap`, Rust's
# `BinaryHeap` and Python's `heapq` are `lists.ledger`'s heap; `HashMap`,
# `HashSet`, Python's `dict`, `set`, `OrderedDict`, `defaultdict`, `Counter`
# and `ChainMap` are `maps.ledger`'s and `sets.ledger`'s, and so is the
# algebra of two `BTreeSet`s. `BTreeMap` and `BTreeSet` stand in those two
# ledgers read as the language's map and set, which refused a range for want
# of an order (`map/range-of-keys`, `set/range`, `set/take-one`); they stand
# here again for the ordered structure that answers them.
#
# Three facts of the language shape every answer. A record takes no type
# parameter, so a structure holding the program's values is a shape of this
# package beside the program's own lists, lent with `@`. A type parameter has
# no `==` and no `<`, so an ordered structure takes its order from the caller
# at each call, and a hashed one finds a key through the program's own
# `{K: i64}` lookup map, the language's map hashing any key. No list shortens
# in place, so every structure keeps a count and reuses its lists' room.

## The ordered map and the ordered set

capability ordered/make  an empty ordered map or ordered set
  heroes  containers.new_ordered_map, containers.new_ordered_set
  go      -
  rust    BTreeMap::new, BTreeMap::default, BTreeSet::new, BTreeSet::default
  python  -
  note    a binary search tree balanced by the sizes of its branches (weight-balanced, Hirai and Yamamoto's delta 3 and gamma 2), where the task named a B-tree or a treap: the sizes rank and select already need make the balance free, and it is deterministic, where a treap's priorities from a fixed seed can be attacked and from a random one are not reproducible. Java's TreeMap and .NET's SortedDictionary are red-black trees, Zig's treap a treap, Odin's container an AVL and a red-black tree

capability ordered/put  a value put under a key of an ordered map, replacing the one held
  heroes  containers.ordered_put
  go      -
